= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For parameters $a_1,\ldots,a_n\in M$, <set-theoretic absoluteness> means agreement of the two membership <first-order structures>:
$$
\boxed{(M,\in)\models\varphi(a_1,\ldots,a_n)\ \Longleftrightarrow\ (N,\in)\models\varphi(a_1,\ldots,a_n).}
$$
This must hold for every such parameter tuple. Equivalently, <formula relativization to a class> produces two formulas $\varphi^M$ and $\varphi^N$ whose truth values agree on tuples from $M$. Agreement for parameters only in $N\setminus M$ is not part of the definition. For a proper <class in set theory>, this notation is understood separately for each fixed <first-order formula>, rather than as an unrestricted truth predicate for the entire universe.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A syntactically <bounded formula in set theory> is built from the atomic <first-order formulas> $u=v$ and $u\in v$ using <Boolean operations> and bounded quantifiers $\exists u\in v$ and $\forall u\in v$. These abbreviate $\exists u(u\in v\land\cdots)$ and $\forall u(u\in v\Rightarrow\cdots)$, with $u$ different from the bounding variable $v$.

The superscript in the question allows provable equivalence: a <ZF-equivalent bounded formula> is any <first-order formula> $\varphi(\vec v)$ for which some syntactically <bounded formula in set theory> $\psi(\vec v)$ satisfies
$$
\boxed{\mathsf{ZF}\vdash\forall\vec v\,(\varphi(\vec v)\leftrightarrow\psi(\vec v)).}
$$
Thus $\Delta_0^{\mathrm{ZF}}$ is the class of <first-order formulas> equivalent over <ZF> to a syntactic $\Delta_0$ <first-order formula>. If a convention uses $\Delta_0^{\mathrm{ZF}}$ just for syntactically bounded set-theoretic <first-order formulas>, it is the smaller syntactic class; the subsequent <set-theoretic absoluteness> proof works for both conventions.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <Von Neumann hierarchy> is the following <transfinite recursion> on the <ordinals>:
$$
\boxed{V_0=\varnothing,\qquad V_{\alpha+1}=\mathcal P(V_\alpha),\qquad V_\lambda=\bigcup_{\beta<\lambda}V_\beta\ \text{for nonzero limit }\lambda.}
$$
Here $\mathcal P$ denotes the full <power set>. The levels are increasing <transitive sets>, and the construction is continuous at <limit ordinals>. <Transfinite induction> proves these properties: transitivity at a successor follows because a member of a <subset> of $V_\alpha$ already belongs to $V_\alpha\subseteq\mathcal P(V_\alpha)$; at a limit it follows from the <set union> of increasing <transitive sets>. In <ZF>, <axiom of foundation> and <axiom schema of replacement> ensure that every <set> belongs to some $V_\alpha$. In terms of the <rank of a set>, $x\in V_\alpha$ exactly when $\operatorname{rank}(x)<\alpha$.

= iv
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

First prove <set-theoretic absoluteness> for syntactically <bounded formulas in set theory> by induction on their construction. Atomic equality and membership use the actual equality and membership relations in both <transitive classes>. <Boolean operations> preserve agreement.

For the bounded existential step, take $a\in M$ and suppose $N\models\exists u\in a\,\psi(u,\vec b)$. Its witness $c$ is an actual member of $a$. Since $M$ is a <transitive class>, $c\in M$; the induction hypothesis then gives $M\models\psi(c,\vec b)$. The converse uses the same witness in $N$. The bounded universal step follows similarly because both structures quantify over exactly the actual members of $a$, or follows by <negation> from the existential step. Hence
$$
\boxed{\psi^M(\vec b)\leftrightarrow\psi^N(\vec b)\qquad\text{for every syntactic }\Delta_0\text{ formula and }\vec b\in M.}
$$
No <ZF> axioms are needed for this syntactic statement beyond the ambient meaning of a <transitive class>. For a <ZF-equivalent bounded formula> $\varphi$, choose a bounded equivalent $\psi$. The assumption that both structures model <ZF> gives $\varphi^M\leftrightarrow\psi^M$ and $\varphi^N\leftrightarrow\psi^N$. Combining these with the displayed equivalence proves the asserted <set-theoretic absoluteness> of $\Delta_0^{\mathrm{ZF}}$.

= v
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

The essential fact is <absoluteness of cardinalhood in limit ranks>. Let $\alpha\leq\beta$ be <limit ordinals> and $x\in V_\alpha$. Being an <ordinal> is absolute between these <transitive sets>, so a non-ordinal is a <cardinal number> in neither structure. For an <ordinal> $\delta\in V_\alpha$, we have $\delta<\alpha$.

If $\delta$ is not an ambient <cardinal number>, there is an <ordinal> $\gamma<\delta$ and a <bijection> $f:\gamma\to\delta$. With <Kuratowski ordered pairs>, its graph has <rank of a set> at most $\delta+3$. Since $\alpha$ is a <limit ordinal>, $\delta+3<\alpha$, so $f\in V_\alpha$. The assertion that $f$ is such a <bijection> is a <bounded formula in set theory> and is therefore evaluated correctly by each rank. Conversely, any internal <bijection> witnessing failure of <cardinal number> status is an actual one by the same <set-theoretic absoluteness>. Consequently
$$
\boxed{V_\alpha\models\text{``}\delta\text{ is a cardinal''}\ \Longleftrightarrow\ \delta\text{ is an ambient cardinal}\ \Longleftrightarrow\ V_\beta\models\text{``}\delta\text{ is a cardinal''.}}
$$
Unlike arbitrary <transitive models>, these rank-initial structures contain every sufficiently low-rank witness. That witness availability is what upgrades the usual <downward absoluteness of cardinalhood> to agreement in both directions.

= vi
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}

Use the standard convention that a <strongly inaccessible cardinal> is uncountable, regular and strong limit. **The printed explanatory definition omits uncountability**: literally it also admits $\kappa=\omega$, for which no finite cardinal is a <worldly cardinal>. Thus that omission makes the requested conclusion false under the literal abbreviated definition. The proof below applies to the usual, intended <strongly inaccessible cardinal> convention $\kappa>\omega$.

First $|V_\gamma|<\kappa$ for every $\gamma<\kappa$. At successor stages this follows from the <strong limit cardinal> property, and at limits from the <regular cardinal> property. Consequently $V_\kappa\models\mathsf{ZFC}$. In particular, for <axiom schema of replacement>, a domain $a\in V_\kappa$ has size less than $\kappa$, so its functional image has fewer than $\kappa$ elements, so the supremum of the <rank of a set> over its elements is below $\kappa$ by <regular cardinal> structure. All other axioms, including <axiom of choice>, have their witnesses at bounded ranks below $\kappa$; uncountability supplies <axiom of infinity>.

Enumerate the <first-order formulas> as $\varphi_0,\varphi_1,\ldots$. For each finite collection, reflection inside the set structure $V_\kappa$ gives a <closed unbounded subset> of $\kappa$ of agreeing ranks. To see why the witness bounds stay below $\kappa$, there are fewer than $\kappa$ parameter tuples at each $V_\gamma$, and the supremum of their least witness ranks remains below $\kappa$ by <regular cardinal> structure. Closing under these bounds and taking increasing countable limits gives the usual <reflection theorem for definable hierarchies> argument. The countable intersection of these <closed unbounded subsets> is still <closed unbounded> because $\kappa$ is regular and uncountable. At each resulting $\alpha$,
$$
(V_\alpha,\in)\prec(V_\kappa,\in),\qquad V_\alpha\models\mathsf{ZFC}.
$$
The infinite <cardinal numbers> below $\kappa$ also form a <closed unbounded subset>: they are unbounded because $\lambda^+\leq2^\lambda<\kappa$ for infinite $\lambda<\kappa$, and a supremum of increasing <cardinal numbers> is a <cardinal number>. Intersect the two <closed unbounded subsets>. Every member of the intersection is a <worldly cardinal>, and an unbounded <subset> of a <regular cardinal> $\kappa$ has size $\kappa$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\left|\{\alpha<\kappa:\alpha\text{ is a cardinal and }V_\alpha\models\mathsf{ZFC}\}\right|=\kappa.}
$$
In fact this proves the stronger <worldly cardinals below an inaccessible cardinal> result that these <worldly cardinals> contain a <closed unbounded subset> of $\kappa$.

